1
00:00:01,838 --> 00:00:06,479
Chapter 3. The spout in.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,999
Entering that gable ended spout in, you found yourself in a wide, low,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,479
straggling entry, with old-fashioned Wayne Scots, reminding one of the

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,678
bullwarks of some condemned old craft.

5
00:00:19,679 --> 00:00:24,879
On one side hung a very large oil painting so thoroughly bes and every way

6
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:29,837
defaced that in the unequal crosslights by which you viewed it, it was only by

7
00:00:29,838 --> 00:00:34,158
diligent study and a series of systematic visits to it and careful

8
00:00:34,159 --> 00:00:38,718
inquiry of the neighbors that you could anyway arrive at an understanding of its

9
00:00:38,719 --> 00:00:44,078
purpose. such unaccountable masses of shades and shadows that at first you

10
00:00:44,079 --> 00:00:48,238
almost thought some ambitious young artist in the time of the New England

11
00:00:48,239 --> 00:00:54,158
hags had endeavored to delineate chaos bewitched. But by dent of much and

12
00:00:54,159 --> 00:00:58,638
earnest contemplation and off-re repeated ponderings, and especially by

13
00:00:58,639 --> 00:01:03,118
throwing open the little window towards the back of the entry, you might at last

14
00:01:03,119 --> 00:01:10,638
come to the conclusion that such an idea, however wild, might not be altogether unwarranted.

15
00:01:10,639 --> 00:01:15,678
But what most puzzled and confounded you was a long, limber, portentious black

16
00:01:15,679 --> 00:01:20,718
mass of something hovering at the center of the picture, over three blue, dim,

17
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:25,999
perpendicular lines floating in a nameless yeast, a bogggy, soggy,

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,638
squitchy picture, truly enough to drive a nervous man distracted. Yet was there

19
00:01:32,639 --> 00:01:37,598
a sort of indefinite half-attained unimaginable sublimity about it that

20
00:01:37,599 --> 00:01:42,317
fairly froze you to it, till you involuntarily took an oath with yourself

21
00:01:42,318 --> 00:01:48,718
to find out what that marvelous painting meant. Ever and a non a bright but alas

22
00:01:48,719 --> 00:01:51,439
deceptive idea would dart you through.

23
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,078
It's the black sea in a midnight gale.

24
00:01:54,079 --> 00:01:59,518
It's the unnatural combat of the four primal elements. It's a blasted heath.

25
00:01:59,519 --> 00:02:04,718
It's a hyperorean winter scene. It's the breaking up of the icebound stream of

26
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:09,837
time. But at last all these fancies yielded to that one portentious

27
00:02:09,838 --> 00:02:14,158
something in the picture's midst that once found out, and all the rest were

28
00:02:14,159 --> 00:02:19,919
plain. But stop. Does it not bear a faint resemblance to a gigantic fish,

29
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:23,198
even the great Leviathan himself?

30
00:02:23,199 --> 00:02:27,999
In fact, the artist's design seemed this, a final theory of my own, partly

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,038
based upon the aggregated opinions of many aged persons with whom I conversed

32
00:02:33,039 --> 00:02:38,558
upon the subject. The picture represents a Cape Horner in a great hurricane, the

33
00:02:38,559 --> 00:02:43,038
half-founded ship weltering there with its three dismantled masts alone

34
00:02:43,039 --> 00:02:48,238
visible, and an exasperated whale purposing to spring clean over the

35
00:02:48,239 --> 00:02:52,718
craft, as in the enormous act of impaling himself upon the three mast

36
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:58,078
heads. The opposite wall of this entry was hung over with a heathenish array of

37
00:02:58,079 --> 00:03:03,038
monstrous clubs and spears. Some were thickly set with glittering teeth

38
00:03:03,039 --> 00:03:08,718
resembling ivory saws. Others were tufted with knots of human hair, and one

39
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:13,439
was sickle-shaped with a vast handle sweeping round like the segment made in

40
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:16,959
the new moan grass by a longarmed mower.

41
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:21,999
You shuddered as you gazed and wondered what monstrous cannibal and savage could

42
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,638
ever have gone at death harvesting with such a hacking, horrifying implement.

43
00:03:26,639 --> 00:03:31,118
Mixed with these were rusty old whailing lances and harpoons, all broken and

44
00:03:31,119 --> 00:03:34,238
deformed. Some were storied weapons.

45
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:39,919
With this once long last, now wildly elbowed, 50 years ago did Nathan Swain

46
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:45,518
kill 15 whales between a sunrise and a sunset. And that harpoon, so like a

47
00:03:45,519 --> 00:03:50,718
corkcrew now, was flung in the Javan seas and run away with by a whale years

48
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:53,837
afterward slain off the Cape of Blanco.

49
00:03:53,838 --> 00:03:59,198
The original iron entered nigh the tail, and like a restless needle sojourning in

50
00:03:59,199 --> 00:04:06,078
the body of a man, traveled full 40 ft, and at last was found embedded in the hump.

51
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:11,598
Crossing this dusky entry and on through yawn low arched way, cut through what in

52
00:04:11,599 --> 00:04:16,158
old times must have been a great central chimney with fireplaces all round, you

53
00:04:16,159 --> 00:04:21,678
enter the public room, a still duskier place as this, with such low, ponderous

54
00:04:21,679 --> 00:04:26,317
beams above, and such old wrinkled planks beneath that you would almost

55
00:04:26,318 --> 00:04:31,598
fancy you trod some old crafts cockpits, especially of such a howling night when

56
00:04:31,599 --> 00:04:38,479
this corner anchored old ark rocked so furiously On one side stood a long, low shelf-like

57
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:43,759
table covered with cracked glass cases, filled with dusty rarities gathered from

58
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:46,399
this wide world's remotest nooks.

59
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:51,118
Projecting from the further angle of the room stands a darkl looking den, the

60
00:04:51,119 --> 00:04:56,638
bar, a rude attempt at a right whale's head. Be that how it may, there stands

61
00:04:56,639 --> 00:05:01,837
the vast arched bone of the whale's jaw, so wide a coach might almost drive

62
00:05:01,838 --> 00:05:06,558
beneath it. Within are shabby shelves ranged round with old decanters,

63
00:05:06,559 --> 00:05:11,598
bottles, flasks, and in those jaws of swift destruction, like another cursed

64
00:05:11,599 --> 00:05:16,479
Jonah, by which name indeed they called him, bustles a little withered old man

65
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:22,158
who, for their money, dearly sells the sailor's deliriums and death.

66
00:05:22,159 --> 00:05:26,558
Abominable are the tumblers into which he pours his poison, though true

67
00:05:26,559 --> 00:05:31,357
cylinders without, within the villainous green goggling glasses deceitfully

68
00:05:31,358 --> 00:05:34,238
tapered downwards to a cheating bottom.

69
00:05:34,239 --> 00:05:38,558
Parallel meridians rudely pecked into the glass surround these footpads

70
00:05:38,559 --> 00:05:43,999
goblets. Fill to this mark, and your charge is but a penny. To this, a penny

71
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,879
more, and so on to the full glass, the cape horn measure which you may gulp

72
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:51,357
down for a shilling.

73
00:05:51,358 --> 00:05:55,118
Upon entering the place, I found a number of young seaman gathered about a

74
00:05:55,119 --> 00:06:00,158
table, examining by a dim light the diverse specimens of scrimshander.

75
00:06:00,159 --> 00:06:04,718
I sought the landlord, and telling him I desired to be accommodated with a room,

76
00:06:04,719 --> 00:06:09,919
received for answer that his house was full, not a bed unoccupied.

77
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:15,038
But have asked, he added, tapping his forehead, you ain't no objections to

78
00:06:15,039 --> 00:06:18,638
sharing a hopaneer's blanket, have you?

79
00:06:18,639 --> 00:06:23,837
I suppose you're going away, Ling, so you better get used to that sort of thing.

80
00:06:23,838 --> 00:06:25,837
I told him that I never like to sleep

81
00:06:25,838 --> 00:06:29,678
too in a bed, that if I should ever do so, it would depend upon who the

82
00:06:29,679 --> 00:06:35,118
harpineer might be, and that if he, the landlord, really had no other place for

83
00:06:35,119 --> 00:06:40,078
me, and the harpooner was not decidedly objectionable, why, rather than wander

84
00:06:40,079 --> 00:06:44,078
further about a strange town on so bitter a night, I would put up with the

85
00:06:44,079 --> 00:06:49,837
half of any decent man's blanket. I thought so. All right, take a seat.

86
00:06:49,838 --> 00:06:54,797
Supper. You want supper? Supper will be ready directly.

87
00:06:54,798 --> 00:06:59,439
I sat down on an old wooden settle carved all over like a bench on the

88
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:04,317
battery. At one end, a ruminating tar was still further adorning it with his

89
00:07:04,318 --> 00:07:08,797
jack knife, stooping over and diligently working away at the space between his

90
00:07:08,798 --> 00:07:13,678
legs. He was trying his hand at a ship under full sail, but he didn't make much

91
00:07:13,679 --> 00:07:19,038
headway, I thought. At last some four or five of us were summoned to our meal in

92
00:07:19,039 --> 00:07:24,718
an adjoining room. It was cold as Iceland, no fire at all. The landlord

93
00:07:24,719 --> 00:07:29,598
said he couldn't afford it. Nothing but two dismal tallow candles, each in a

94
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:34,638
winding sheet. We were fain to button up our monkey jackets and hold to our lips

95
00:07:34,639 --> 00:07:39,759
cups of scalding tea with our half frozen fingers. But the fair was of the

96
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:44,797
most substantial kind. Not only meat and potatoes, but dumplings. Good heavens.

97
00:07:44,798 --> 00:07:49,678
dumplings for supper. One young fellow in a green box coat addressed himself to

98
00:07:49,679 --> 00:07:53,678
these dumplings in a most direful manner.

99
00:07:53,679 --> 00:07:58,718
My boy, said the landlord, you'll have the nightmare to a dead certainty.

100
00:07:58,719 --> 00:08:04,879
Landlord, I whispered, "That ain't the harpooner, is it?" "Oh no," said he,

101
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:11,357
looking sort of diabolically funny. "The harpineer is a dark complexion chap. He

102
00:08:11,358 --> 00:08:16,158
never eats dumplings. He don't. He eats nothing but steaks and he likes them

103
00:08:16,159 --> 00:08:22,797
rare. The devil he does, says I. Where is that harpooner? Is he here? He'll be

104
00:08:22,798 --> 00:08:27,999
here for long was the answer. I could not help it, but I began to feel

105
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,879
suspicious of this dark complexioned harpooner. At any rate, I made up my

106
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:36,718
mind that if it so turned out that we should sleep together, he must undress

107
00:08:36,719 --> 00:08:39,919
and get into bed before I did.

108
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:44,317
Supper over. The company went back to the barroom. when knowing not what else

109
00:08:44,318 --> 00:08:49,439
to do with myself, I resolved to spend the rest of the evening as a looker on.

110
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,678
Presently a rioting noise was heard without. Starting up, the landlord

111
00:08:53,679 --> 00:08:58,238
cried, "That's the grandpas' crew. I seed her reported in the offing this

112
00:08:58,239 --> 00:09:02,959
morning. A three years voyage and a full ship. Harrah, boys, now we'll have the

113
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,598
latest news from the Fiji's."

114
00:09:05,599 --> 00:09:09,759
A tramping of seab boots was heard in the entry. The door was flung open, and

115
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:14,879
inrolled a wild set of mariners enough, enveloped in their shaggy watch coats,

116
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:19,279
and with their heads muffled in woolen comforters, all bearned and ragged, and

117
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:23,439
their beards stiff with icicles, they seemed an eruption of bears from

118
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:27,678
Labrador. They had just landed from their boat, and this was the first house

119
00:09:27,679 --> 00:09:31,837
they entered. No wonder then that they made a straight wake for the whale's

120
00:09:31,838 --> 00:09:37,518
mouth the bar when the wrinkled little old Jonah there officiating soon poured

121
00:09:37,519 --> 00:09:42,399
them out brimmers all round. One complained of a bad cold in his head,

122
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:47,439
upon which Jonah mixed him a pitch-like potion of gin and molasses, which he

123
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:52,797
swore was a sovereign cure for all colds and catters, whatever, never mind of how

124
00:09:52,798 --> 00:09:57,279
longstanding, or whether caught off the coast of Labrador or on the weather side

125
00:09:57,280 --> 00:10:02,078
of an ice island. The liquor soon mounted into their heads, as it

126
00:10:02,079 --> 00:10:06,879
generally does, even with the errantest toppers newly landed from sea, and they

127
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:10,158
began capering about most obstreporously.

128
00:10:10,159 --> 00:10:14,638
I observed, however, that one of them held somewhat aloof, and though he

129
00:10:14,639 --> 00:10:18,558
seemed desirous not to spoil the hilarity of his shipmates by his own

130
00:10:18,559 --> 00:10:22,638
sober face, yet upon the whole he refrained from making as much noise as

131
00:10:22,639 --> 00:10:27,198
the rest, this man interested me at once, and since the sea gods had

132
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:31,759
ordained that he should soon be my shipmate, though a sleeping partner one,

133
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:35,598
so far as this narrative is concerned, I will here venture upon a little

134
00:10:35,599 --> 00:10:41,038
description of him. He stood a full 6 ft in height with noble shoulders and a

135
00:10:41,039 --> 00:10:46,718
chest like a coffer dam. I have seldom seen such brawn in a man. His face was

136
00:10:46,719 --> 00:10:51,837
deeply brown and burnt, making his white teeth dazzling by the contrast, while in

137
00:10:51,838 --> 00:10:56,317
the deep shadows of his eyes floated some reminiscences that did not seem to

138
00:10:56,318 --> 00:11:01,357
give him much joy. His voice at once announced that he was a southerner, and

139
00:11:01,358 --> 00:11:05,598
from his fine stature I thought he must be one of those tall mountaineers from

140
00:11:05,599 --> 00:11:10,879
the Alaganian Ridge in Virginia. When the realry of his companions had mounted

141
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:15,439
to its height, this man slipped away unobserved, and I saw no more of him

142
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:20,479
till he became my comrade on the sea. In a few minutes, however, he was missed by

143
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:25,678
his shipmates, and being, it seems, for some reason, a huge favorite with them,

144
00:11:25,679 --> 00:11:27,999
they raised a cry of, "Bulkington!

145
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,999
Bulkington! Where's Bulkington?" and darted out of the house in pursuit of him.

146
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,558
It was now about 9:00, and the room seeming almost supernaturally quiet

147
00:11:38,559 --> 00:11:42,879
after these orgies, I began to congratulate myself upon a little plan

148
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:47,837
that had occurred to me just previous to the entrance of the seaman.

149
00:11:47,838 --> 00:11:52,959
No man prefers to sleep too in a bed. In fact, you would a good deal rather not

150
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:57,357
sleep with your own brother. I don't know how it is, but people like to be

151
00:11:57,358 --> 00:12:01,279
private when they are sleeping. And when it comes to sleeping with an unknown

152
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:06,959
stranger in a strange inn in a strange town, and that stranger a harpooner,

153
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:11,678
then your objections indefinitely multiply. Nor was there any earthly

154
00:12:11,679 --> 00:12:16,718
reason why I, as a sailor, should sleep two in a bed more than anybody else. For

155
00:12:16,719 --> 00:12:21,919
sailors no more sleep two in a bed at sea than bachelor kings do ashore. To be

156
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:26,158
sure they all sleep together in one apartment. But you have your own hammock

157
00:12:26,159 --> 00:12:31,439
and cover yourself with your own blanket and sleep in your own skin. The more I

158
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:35,357
pondered over this harpooner, the more I abominated the thought of sleeping with

159
00:12:35,358 --> 00:12:40,399
him. It was fair to presume that being a harpooner his linen or woolen as the

160
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:45,198
case might be would not be of the tidiest, certainly none of the finest. I

161
00:12:45,199 --> 00:12:49,518
began to twitch all over. Besides, it was getting late, and my decent

162
00:12:49,519 --> 00:12:53,999
harpooner ought to be home and going bedwards. Suppose now he should tumble

163
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,238
in upon me at midnight. How could I tell from what vile hole he had been coming?

164
00:13:00,239 --> 00:13:04,718
Landlord, I've changed my mind about that harpineer. I shant sleep with him.

165
00:13:04,719 --> 00:13:07,357
I'll try the bench here.

166
00:13:07,358 --> 00:13:12,479
Just as you please. I'm sorry I can't spare you a tablecloth for the mattress

167
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:17,678
and it's a plaguy rough board here feeling of the knots and notches but

168
00:13:17,679 --> 00:13:22,638
wait a bit scrimshander I've got a carpenters's plane there in the bar wait

169
00:13:22,639 --> 00:13:28,078
I say and I'll make you snug enough so saying he procured the plane and with

170
00:13:28,079 --> 00:13:33,118
his old silk handkerchief first dusting the bench vigorously set to planing away

171
00:13:33,119 --> 00:13:38,558
at my bed but while grinning like an ape the shavings flew right and left till At

172
00:13:38,559 --> 00:13:43,439
last the plain iron came bump against an indestructible knot. The landlord was

173
00:13:43,440 --> 00:13:47,518
near spraining his wrist, and I told him, for heaven's sake, to quit. The bed

174
00:13:47,519 --> 00:13:50,959
was soft enough to suit me, and I did not know how all the planing in the

175
00:13:50,960 --> 00:13:55,837
world could make ider down of a pine plank. So gathering up the shavings with

176
00:13:55,838 --> 00:13:59,518
another grin, and throwing them into the great stove in the middle of the room,

177
00:13:59,519 --> 00:14:03,919
he went about his business, and left me in a brown study.

178
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:08,399
I now took the measure of the bench and found that it was a foot too short, but

179
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:12,558
that could be mended with a chair, but it was a foot too narrow, and the other

180
00:14:12,559 --> 00:14:16,959
bench in the room was about 4 in higher than the planained one, so there was no

181
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:21,919
yoking them. I then placed the first bench lengthwise along the only clear

182
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:26,558
space against the wall, leaving a little interval between for my back to settle

183
00:14:26,559 --> 00:14:31,837
down in. But I soon found that there came such a draft of cold air over me

184
